WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.520
In today’s big data context, the previous&nbsp;
approaches are either incomplete or suboptimal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.720
For example, the SEMMA methodology&nbsp;
disregards completely data collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.960
and preprocessing of different data sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.480
These stages normally constitute most of&nbsp;
the work in a successful big data project.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.640
We begin by realizing that there are two sides&nbsp;
to the analytics life cycle – discovery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.680
deployment. Let’s take a look at the tasks&nbsp;
for both sides and see how they interact to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.640
create an iterative process that you can use to&nbsp;
produce repeatable, reliable predictive results.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:42.476
The Discovery Phase of the Analytics Life Cycle

00:00:42.476 --> 00:00:45.920
The first phase is the discovery&nbsp;
phase of the analytics life cycle:

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.000
• Ask a question. The discovery process&nbsp;
is driven by asking business questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.320
that produce innovations. So, the first step&nbsp;
is defining what the business needs to know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.040
And then translating the business question into&nbsp;
a mathematical representation of the problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.800
which can be solved with predictive analytics.&nbsp;
Business people also need to specify the need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.880
scope, market conditions and goals related&nbsp;
to a business question they are trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.720
answer – which helps in the selection of&nbsp;
the most appropriate modeling techniques.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:23.760
• Prepare the data. Technologies like Hadoop&nbsp;
and faster, cheaper computers have made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.080
possible to store and use more data, and&nbsp;
more types of data, than ever before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.960
But this has only amplified the need&nbsp;
to join data in different formats from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.920
different sources and transform raw data so that&nbsp;
it can be used as input for predictive modeling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.080
With new data types from connected devices,&nbsp;
such as machine sensor data or web logs from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.880
online interactions, the data preparation&nbsp;
stage has become even more challenging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.640
Many organizations still report that they spend&nbsp;
an inordinate amount of time, sometimes up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.480
80 percent, dealing with data preparation&nbsp;
tasks. The majority of time in the discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.360
phase should be spent on exploring data and&nbsp;
creating good models instead of preparing data.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:13.040
• Explore the data. Interactive, self-service&nbsp;
visualization tools need to serve a wide range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.640
of users, from the business analyst&nbsp;
with no statistical knowledge to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.440
the analytically savvy data scientist, so&nbsp;
they can easily search for relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.120
trends and patterns to gain deeper understanding&nbsp;
of the data. In this step, the question and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.960
approach formed in the initial “ask” phase of the&nbsp;
project will be refined. Ideas on how to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.960
the problem from an analytical perspective are&nbsp;
developed and tested. While examining the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:44.480
you may find the need to add, delete or combine&nbsp;
variables to create more precisely focused models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.200
Fast, interactive tools help&nbsp;
make this an iterative process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.480
which is crucial for identifying&nbsp;
the best questions and answers.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.520
• Model the data. In this stage, numerous&nbsp;
analytical and machine-learning modeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.160
algorithms are applied to the data to find the&nbsp;
best representation of the relationships in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.000
data that will help answer the business question.&nbsp;
Analytical tools search for a combination of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.920
and modeling techniques that reliably predict&nbsp;
a desired outcome. There is no single algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.680
that always performs best. The “best” algorithm&nbsp;
for solving the problem depends on the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.760
Experimentation is key to finding the most&nbsp;
reliable answer, and automated model building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:34.320
can help minimize the time to results and boost&nbsp;
the productivity of analytical teams. In the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.680
data miners and data scientists were only able&nbsp;
to create several models in a week or month using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.840
manual model-building tools. Improved software and&nbsp;
faster computers have sped up the model-building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.480
process so hundreds or even thousands of&nbsp;
models can be created today in the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:57.040
frame. But that brings to the surface another&nbsp;
issue. How to quickly and reliably identify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.200
one model that performs best? Model “tournaments”&nbsp;
provide a way to compare many different competing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.080
algorithms with the opportunity to choose the one&nbsp;
that provides the best results for a specific set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.800
of data. With automated tournaments of&nbsp;
machine-learning algorithms and clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.280
defined metrics to identify the champion model,&nbsp;
this has become an easier process. Analysts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.400
data scientists can spend their time focusing&nbsp;
on more strategic questions and investigations.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:26.236
The Deployment Phase of the Analytics Life Cycle

00:04:26.236 --> 00:04:29.680
The second phase is the deployment&nbsp;
phase of the analytics life cycle:

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.920
• Implement your models. This is where you take&nbsp;
the insights learned from the discovery phase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:42.080
and put them into action using repeatable,&nbsp;
automated processes. In many organizations this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.720
the point where the analytical modeling process&nbsp;
slows dramatically because there is no defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.440
transition between discovery and deployment,&nbsp;
or collaboration between the model developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.920
and IT deployment architects, much less optimized&nbsp;
automation. In most organizations the deployment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.720
environment is very different from the discovery&nbsp;
environment, especially when the predictive models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:09.040
are supporting operational decision making. Often,&nbsp;
IT has to apply rigorous governance policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.320
to this environment to ensure service-level&nbsp;
agreements with the business. By integrating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.760
discovery and deployment phases, you can create&nbsp;
an automated, flexible and repeatable transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:26.000
that improves operational decisions. Additionally,&nbsp;
a transparent, governed process is important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.120
everyone – especially auditors. Once built, the&nbsp;
model is registered, tested or validated, approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.840
and declared ready to be used with production&nbsp;
data (embedded into operational systems).

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:45.040
• Act on new information. There are two types of&nbsp;
decisions that can be made based on analytical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:51.520
results. Strategic decisions are made by humans&nbsp;
who examine results and take action, usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.440
looking to the future. Operational decisions are&nbsp;
automated – like credit scores or recommended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.120
best offers. They don’t require human intervention&nbsp;
because the rules that humans would apply can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.120
coded into the production systems. More and more&nbsp;
organizations are looking to automate operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.200
decisions and provide real-time results to&nbsp;
reduce decision latencies. Basing operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.720
decisions on answers from analytical models also&nbsp;
makes these decisions objective, consistent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.960
repeatable and measurable. Integration of models&nbsp;
with enterprise decision management tools enables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.480
organizations to build comprehensive and&nbsp;
complete operational decision flows. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.680
combine analytical models with business-rule-based&nbsp;
triggers to produce the best automated decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.320
And because this is formally defined&nbsp;
within the decision management tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.840
updates and refinements to changing conditions are&nbsp;
easy – improving business agility and governance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.800
Once approved for production, the decision&nbsp;
management tool applies the model to new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.280
operational data, generating the predictive&nbsp;
insights necessary for better actions.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:05.040
• Evaluate your results. The next and perhaps&nbsp;
most important step is to evaluate the outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.560
of the actions produced by the analytical model.&nbsp;
Did your models produce the correct predictions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.160
Were tangible results realized, such as&nbsp;
increased revenue or decreased costs?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.320
With continuous monitoring and measurement of the&nbsp;
models’ performance based on standardized metrics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.280
you can evaluate the success of&nbsp;
these assets for your business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.760
That evaluation can then feed&nbsp;
the next iteration of the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:36.000
creating a continuous machine-learning loop. If&nbsp;
you identify degradation of analytical models, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.040
can define the optimal strategy to refresh them&nbsp;
so they continue to produce the desired results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.200
With increasing numbers of analytical models,&nbsp;
automation is necessary to quickly identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.960
models that need the most attention,&nbsp;
and even deliver automated retraining.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:59.360
• Ask again. Predictive models are not forever.&nbsp;
The factors that drive the predictions in a model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.040
change over time, your customers change over time,&nbsp;
competitors enter or leave the market, and new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:11.040
data becomes available. You have to refresh even&nbsp;
the most accurate models, and organizations will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.880
need to go through the discovery and deployment&nbsp;
steps again. It’s a constant and evolving process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:23.040
If a model degrades, it is recalibrated by&nbsp;
changing the model coefficients or rebuilt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.360
existing and new characteristics. When the model&nbsp;
no longer serves a business need, it is retired.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.080
In summary, in an effective analytical&nbsp;
environment, data is rapidly created and accessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:42.320
in the correct structure for exploration and model&nbsp;
development. Models are rapidly built and tested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.600
and deployed into a production environment&nbsp;
with minimal delay. Production models quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:53.840
generate trusted output. Model performance is&nbsp;
constantly monitored, and underperforming models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:00.160
are quickly replaced by more up-to-date models. In&nbsp;
short, a successful analytics strategy means more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.280
than creating a powerfully predictive model; it&nbsp;
is about managing each of these lifecycle stages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.520
holistically for a particular model and across&nbsp;
the entire portfolio of models. This is no easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.960
feat. Consider that analysts and data scientists&nbsp;
don’t just develop one model to solve a problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.880
They develop a set of competing models and use&nbsp;
different techniques to address complex problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.680
They will have models at various stages&nbsp;
of development and models tailored for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.840
different product lines and business problems.&nbsp;
An organization can quickly find itself managing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:40.160
thousands of models. Furthermore, the model&nbsp;
environment is anything but static. Models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.040
will be continually updated as they are tested&nbsp;
and as new results and data become available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:58.320
The goal is to build the best predictive&nbsp;
models possible, using the best data available.

